**Line:** $y=mx+c$, or a rearrangement like $x+2y=6$, has $x$ and $y$ to the power $1$ and a straight graph. Equations such as $x=-3$ give vertical straight lines; equations such as $y=6$ give horizontal straight lines.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw[->] (-3.2,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2)--(0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->] (-2.8,-1.7)--(2.8,2.8);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Parabola:** $y=ax^{2}+bx+c$, with $a\ne0$, has highest power $2$ in $x$, giving a U-shape opening up when $a>0$ and down when $a<0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw[->] (-3.2,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2)--(0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-1.2:2.2,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x-0.5)*(\x-0.5)-1.5});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Exponential:** $y=a^{x}$ has $x$ as the **exponent**, with $a>0$ and $a\ne1$. It increases for $a>1$ and decreases for $0<a<1$; these directions are reversed for $y=a^{-x}$. Both have the $x$-axis as a horizontal asymptote. Multiplying by a negative number reflects the curve in the $x$-axis, and adding a constant moves it vertically.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw[->] (-3.2,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2)--(0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,smooth,domain=-3:1.55,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(2))});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
